1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,480

2
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:10,040
Today, it's our pleasure to host
Nouamane Tazi from Hugging Face.

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:13,040
He's the lead author of
the Ultra-Scale Playbook

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,440
and a core developer
of Nanotronics,

5
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:19,920
Hugging Face's open source
distributed training library.

6
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:22,760
His work expands projects
like Star StarCoder 2,

7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:25,400
Small LLM 3, and mixture
of expert scaling

8
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:26,620
with several initiatives.

9
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,080
And he is passionate about
making large scale training

10
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:30,880
practical and accessible.

11
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:34,720
So without further ado,
let me hand it off to him.

12
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:36,460
Thank you so much
for the invitation.

13
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:39,000

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,400
So hello, everyone.

15
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,360
My name is Nouamane Tazi.

16
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:46,760
I've been working with Hugging
Face now for four years.

17
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:49,240
And the talk for today
is Scaling Training

18
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:53,320
to Thousands of GPUs.

19
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:58,960
So today's talk is going to
be mainly inspired by the book

20
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:00,600
that Hugging Face made.

21
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,120
It's called
Ultra-Scale Playbook.

22
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,400
So make sure to check it out.

23
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,740
It's available online and
there is a printed version

24
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:07,660
if you need it.

25
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:10,220
But I also added some
elements since the book now

26
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:12,700
dates from one year
ago, especially

27
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,340
regarding MOE scaling
and some new things

28
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:20,020
that we can take a look at.

29
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:23,620
So without further ado, let's
start with the first question

30
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:25,620
is, why does scaling matter?

31
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:29,060
And if we take a look at the
latest releases of the latest

32
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:33,220
LLMs, we can see that
the trend is still

33
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:35,260
to train larger and larger.

34
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,780
And why is that?

35
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:38,660
That's also because
we can see that there

36
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:41,160
is a correlation
with intelligence.

37
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:45,960
It seems that the bigger the
LLMs are, the smarter they are.

38
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:50,060
And there's just an example
from this week, Kimi K2.6,

39
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:55,020
which is also one
trillion parameter model.

40
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:59,170
So to take just some
numbers, models of these days

41
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:03,290
are one trillion parameter
large, or even larger.

42
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:07,010
They get trained on 15
trillion training tokens

43
00:02:07,010 --> 00:02:11,730
and for context lengths
as far as one million.

44
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:15,650
So how can we do that?

45
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:20,210
Now, to take some quick
overview about what

46
00:02:20,210 --> 00:02:24,650
does this entail from
a infra point of view.

47
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:27,910
So if you want to train
this kind of model,

48
00:02:27,910 --> 00:02:29,610
first, you need to
load the data, which

49
00:02:29,610 --> 00:02:31,490
is about 15 trillion.

50
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:34,390
Imagine that's stored
on a storage somewhere,

51
00:02:34,390 --> 00:02:36,010
and you need to load
that continuously

52
00:02:36,010 --> 00:02:37,650
in your training loop.

53
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:40,530
And each training
iteration, even if the model

54
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:42,830
is as big as one
trillion parameter model,

55
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:45,410
needs to take about one second.

56
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:49,890
And you're limited by the
memory within each GPU or TPU

57
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:51,930
or whatever you're using.

58
00:02:51,930 --> 00:02:54,130
And you need to save
checkpoints to that storage

59
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:55,330
every now and then.

60
00:02:55,330 --> 00:03:00,710
So you're putting a lot of
pressure into these GPUs.

61
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:03,470
So the talk of today is
going to be mainly focused

62
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:09,870
on training, the training
iteration or the training.

63
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:12,870
So let's start
quickly on one GPU.

64
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:13,830
So there is a model.

65
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:15,190
We do forward, backward.

66
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:18,410
We compute gradients, and
then we do the optimizer step

67
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:20,350
and we have an updated model.

68
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:23,350
But for our case, we usually
have global batch size

69
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:28,050
of one million to 10 million,
even like 50 million tokens.

70
00:03:28,050 --> 00:03:31,770
So that, of course, doesn't fit
on a single training iteration.

71
00:03:31,770 --> 00:03:35,250
So what we do usually is we
can do gradient accumulation.

72
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:38,310
So we're going to divide the
batch size to smaller batch

73
00:03:38,310 --> 00:03:38,810
sizes.

74
00:03:38,810 --> 00:03:41,410
And we're going to do multiple
forward and backwards.

75
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:45,855
And we're going to accumulate
gradients sequentially.

76
00:03:45,855 --> 00:03:47,230
The problem with
this approach is

77
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:50,250
we're going to assume that the
model already fits in memory,

78
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:52,230
which is not always the case.

79
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:54,050
Models now are so
big, one trillion.

80
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:55,550
And we said that
we're still limited

81
00:03:55,550 --> 00:04:00,930
by the available VRAM in each
accelerator you're going to use.

82
00:04:00,930 --> 00:04:04,050
So how can we solve
this with more GPUs?

83
00:04:04,050 --> 00:04:07,570
The other thing is that
if you train sequentially

84
00:04:07,570 --> 00:04:11,450
with this approach, you're going
to need to wait for a long time.

85
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:15,130
So if we have more GPUs,
how can we speed this up?

86
00:04:15,130 --> 00:04:18,670
And for this, there is multiple
forms of parallelism we can use.

87
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:21,269
And each one of them is
used in a specific use case.

88
00:04:21,269 --> 00:04:24,410
And hopefully today, you can
get at least an intuition

89
00:04:24,410 --> 00:04:27,730
which parallelism you can
use for which use case.

90
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:31,210
And to help you--

91
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:32,770
so basically for
today, I'm going

92
00:04:32,770 --> 00:04:35,370
to mainly focus on the
first two parallelisms

93
00:04:35,370 --> 00:04:39,770
because they're going to explain
just the intuition of how we can

94
00:04:39,770 --> 00:04:41,650
think about these parallelism.

95
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:46,050
And the final three, we're going
to just quickly skim over them

96
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:48,590
because there's not enough time.

97
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:54,570
And I think that the
approach can be generalized.

98
00:04:54,570 --> 00:05:00,040
So before starting, a few notes.

99
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:04,140
So this approach can be applied
to an accelerator, GPUs, TPUs.

100
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,080
So in the slides, I
use GPUs, but the same

101
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:07,880
can be applied for anything.

102
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:11,320
And it's useful for
pre-training, post-training,

103
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:13,660
distillation
training, inference.

104
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:18,100
So any type of workload can
benefit from these parallelisms.

105
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:19,600
And it's applicable.

106
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,620
You don't need 1,000 GPUs
to benefit from this.

107
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:24,840
As long as you
have two, at least,

108
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:28,160
you can benefit from
these parallelism.

109
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:30,740
So naively, what we want to do?

110
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:32,580
So we said that the
model doesn't fit.

111
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:35,420
So ideally, we want to be
able to shard the model,

112
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:37,300
so either vertically
or horizontally.

113
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:39,120
And that means we're
going to also shard

114
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:43,980
the gradients and the
optimizer and shard the data.

115
00:05:43,980 --> 00:05:46,080
Because we don't
want to wait too long

116
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:49,240
to train sequentially on data.

117
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:51,238
So let's deep dive into scaling.

118
00:05:51,238 --> 00:05:53,780
And first of all, we're going
to start with data parallelism.

119
00:05:53,780 --> 00:05:55,820
And I'm assuming this
is the basic form

120
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:58,760
that everyone is using.

121
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:00,720
So I'm going to go
quickly over it.

122
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:02,260
So instead of
training sequentially

123
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:07,440
over multiple batches, I'm going
to feed each batch to a GPU.

124
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:10,120
So I'm going to have
different batches per GPU.

125
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:11,820
Thus, the name data parallelism.

126
00:06:11,820 --> 00:06:14,180
I'm going to shard the data.

127
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:17,600
And of course, if I have
different data per GPU,

128
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:21,540
each GPU is going to
compute different gradients.

129
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:25,320
And to keep the same model
duplicated on each GPU,

130
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:27,840
I'm going to need to
synchronize these gradients.

131
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:29,200
And how am I going to do that?

132
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:31,680
It's going to be the same
as accumulating gradient,

133
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:34,020
but it's going to be in
a distributed fashion.

134
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:37,860
Thus, we're going to need to
use the AllReduce collective

135
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:41,380
operation, which is
just a distributed

136
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:44,460
sum over multiple GPUs.

137
00:06:44,460 --> 00:06:47,360
So we end up with
the same gradients,

138
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:52,140
and thus, we can do the same
optimizer step over all GPUs.

139
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:54,940
And if you want to take
a look at the code,

140
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:56,660
it's going to look like this.

141
00:06:56,660 --> 00:06:59,880
We can use the DDP
class by PyTorch.

142
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:02,220
And when you wrap
your model with it,

143
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:03,920
when you do the
forward and backward,

144
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:09,680
it automatically handles the
AllReduce part in the backward.

145
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:12,960
So there is a small
problem with this.

146
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:18,060
When we do the AllReduce, the
GPU stay idle in that time.

147
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:23,560
And for that, there is a very
nice tool called torch.profiler

148
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:27,580
that helps you visualize what
the timeline is basically,

149
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:30,240
or the workflow for
the CPU and GPU.

150
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:33,000
And for example, if we apply
it on a naive implementation

151
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,720
of DDP, we can see that there
is a CPU stream that is forward

152
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:37,480
and backward.

153
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:40,120
And then there is the GPU
streams, GPU computation

154
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:41,960
and communication.

155
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:42,980
So there is two things.

156
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:45,880
So the biggest
thing that we want

157
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:48,440
is we don't want
GPU to stay idle.

158
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:51,990
So we definitely want the
GPU computation stream

159
00:07:51,990 --> 00:07:56,870
to be always full, which
is not the case in here,

160
00:07:56,870 --> 00:07:59,390
as we can see here.

161
00:07:59,390 --> 00:08:01,270
The other thing is
we need to make sure

162
00:08:01,270 --> 00:08:03,890
that the CPU is always
ahead of the GPU,

163
00:08:03,890 --> 00:08:06,910
so that we can always
schedule kernels beforehand.

164
00:08:06,910 --> 00:08:10,790
And the third thing is, for
example, in this case of DDP,

165
00:08:10,790 --> 00:08:13,670
we can see that the
AllReduce is not overlapped

166
00:08:13,670 --> 00:08:14,770
with the computation.

167
00:08:14,770 --> 00:08:19,310
So ideally, we want to
overlap this communication

168
00:08:19,310 --> 00:08:21,390
with computation.

169
00:08:21,390 --> 00:08:26,630
So this is a simplified
diagram of the previous.

170
00:08:26,630 --> 00:08:32,230
So how can we overlap
this AllReduce?

171
00:08:32,230 --> 00:08:36,510
We can just start
AllReduce in the gradient

172
00:08:36,510 --> 00:08:40,150
once a bucket of the
gradients is computed.

173
00:08:40,150 --> 00:08:42,549
Basically when you
do the forward,

174
00:08:42,549 --> 00:08:46,470
you start doing the backward
from the last layers.

175
00:08:46,470 --> 00:08:50,610
So you don't need to do the
backward of the entire model

176
00:08:50,610 --> 00:08:52,670
to be able to AllReduce them.

177
00:08:52,670 --> 00:08:54,770
So once, for
example, you compute

178
00:08:54,770 --> 00:08:56,530
the gradient of
the last layer, you

179
00:08:56,530 --> 00:08:58,590
can already start the AllReduce.

180
00:08:58,590 --> 00:09:00,450
And this is what
we do in practice.

181
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:02,930
So for example, if
2 is the last layer,

182
00:09:02,930 --> 00:09:06,230
once the gradients of the
last layer is computed,

183
00:09:06,230 --> 00:09:10,810
we can AllReduce it and we can
continue doing the backward.

184
00:09:10,810 --> 00:09:16,170
And this bucket can-- we
control the size of this bucket

185
00:09:16,170 --> 00:09:22,210
in PyTorch via this argument
bucket cup micro-batch--

186
00:09:22,210 --> 00:09:23,370
microbits.

187
00:09:23,370 --> 00:09:26,890
So you can control how big or
how small the bucket should

188
00:09:26,890 --> 00:09:30,730
be, so that you have a better
overlap of communication

189
00:09:30,730 --> 00:09:32,090
and computation.

190
00:09:32,090 --> 00:09:36,450
And this is what goes through
the mind of any engineer who

191
00:09:36,450 --> 00:09:37,670
tries to scale models.

192
00:09:37,670 --> 00:09:39,930
Basically, when you
have a lot of GPUs,

193
00:09:39,930 --> 00:09:44,090
you definitely want to overlap
efficiently your computation

194
00:09:44,090 --> 00:09:48,570
with communication so that
you avoid any idle time here

195
00:09:48,570 --> 00:09:53,030
for the GPU computation
and to benefit

196
00:09:53,030 --> 00:09:56,130
of the tensor cores of GPUs.

197
00:09:56,130 --> 00:10:00,830
So this is the end goal
of this presentation.

198
00:10:00,830 --> 00:10:03,410
So quickly, you can
go over a recap of DP.

199
00:10:03,410 --> 00:10:05,310
So it's easy to implement.

200
00:10:05,310 --> 00:10:07,490
It's very efficiently
overlapped.

201
00:10:07,490 --> 00:10:08,790
It's really good.

202
00:10:08,790 --> 00:10:10,530
And it's model agnostic, right?

203
00:10:10,530 --> 00:10:12,430
So we can just wrap
any model and it's

204
00:10:12,430 --> 00:10:16,710
going to automatically reduce
the gradients in the backward.

205
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:19,030
But the problem is
that the optimizer step

206
00:10:19,030 --> 00:10:20,690
is duplicated across GPUs.

207
00:10:20,690 --> 00:10:24,210
Because if you have the same
model in each of your GPU,

208
00:10:24,210 --> 00:10:27,630
all of them are going
to do the same optimizer

209
00:10:27,630 --> 00:10:29,430
step, which is bad.

210
00:10:29,430 --> 00:10:30,910
It's duplicated work.

211
00:10:30,910 --> 00:10:34,870
The second thing is that
if you scale more and more

212
00:10:34,870 --> 00:10:36,670
in the number of
GPUs, you're also

213
00:10:36,670 --> 00:10:39,350
going to need more and
more batches of data.

214
00:10:39,350 --> 00:10:41,570
And this is what we
call global batch size.

215
00:10:41,570 --> 00:10:44,790
So your global batch size
is also going to scale.

216
00:10:44,790 --> 00:10:48,660
But of course, you can't
just scale indefinitely.

217
00:10:48,660 --> 00:10:52,060
We're limited by tens
of millions of tokens.

218
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:54,322
And so you can just
apply DP-- if you have,

219
00:10:54,322 --> 00:10:55,780
I don't know, one
million GPUs, you

220
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:59,900
can just use the DP
over all of your GPUs.

221
00:10:59,900 --> 00:11:02,500
And the third thing, which
is the biggest bottleneck,

222
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:07,280
is that DP assumes that the
training step fits in memory.

223
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:09,500
And this is not always
the case, unfortunately.

224
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:13,640
And so we need other
approaches to optimize memory.

225
00:11:13,640 --> 00:11:17,020
And we can look at the
first one, which is DP ZeRO.

226
00:11:17,020 --> 00:11:21,500
So from the name,
DP ZeRO 1 tries

227
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:24,440
to solve the optimizer step.

228
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:26,860
Basically, we said
that in DP we're

229
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:29,140
going to duplicate the
model and all of them

230
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:31,120
are going to do the
same optimizer step.

231
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:34,820
So how can we reduce the memory
at least of the optimizer states

232
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:38,780
so that they're not
duplicated and each GPU can

233
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:44,900
do a different part of the
optimizer-- of the optimization?

234
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:52,480
So the simplest approach is
instead of having the optimizer

235
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:55,160
states of all the
models, I'm going

236
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:57,680
to shard them across my GPUs.

237
00:11:57,680 --> 00:11:59,600
And when I do the
backward step, I'm

238
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:03,520
only going to reduce
scatter the gradients.

239
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:05,460
So I'm not going to have
all the reduce here.

240
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:07,220
I'm only going to
have reduce scatter.

241
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:10,540
And then I'm going to do the
optimizer step for each shard.

242
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:12,580
And this is what's
really good about ZeRO 1.

243
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:14,800
We're not going to
have duplicated work.

244
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:20,560
And once I have my
updated parameter shards,

245
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:24,120
I need to all gather them
before the next step.

246
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:27,880
So this is ZeRO 1 in brief.

247
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:31,800
And there is s very
important intuition

248
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:33,400
that you can learn from this.

249
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:37,040
So before in Vanilla DP, we had
an AllReduce in the backward,

250
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:37,540
right?

251
00:12:37,540 --> 00:12:39,240
In here, we had an AllReduce.

252
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:42,400
And the trick that we
made for ZeRO 1 to work

253
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:46,910
is that we replaced the
AllReduce with a reduce scatter

254
00:12:46,910 --> 00:12:48,510
and an all gather.

255
00:12:48,510 --> 00:12:51,530
And this works great.

256
00:12:51,530 --> 00:12:54,570
And we're going to reuse
this trick in the future.

257
00:12:54,570 --> 00:12:55,190
Why?

258
00:12:55,190 --> 00:12:59,870
Because AllReduce, essentially,
is just a reduce-scatter

259
00:12:59,870 --> 00:13:03,590
plus an all-gather.

260
00:13:03,590 --> 00:13:07,950
So basically, we didn't
add any communication.

261
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:09,610
It's the same communication.

262
00:13:09,610 --> 00:13:12,750
It's just that instead
of doing the AllReduce,

263
00:13:12,750 --> 00:13:15,990
I did reduce-scatter
first so that-- to compute

264
00:13:15,990 --> 00:13:17,670
the gradients first.

265
00:13:17,670 --> 00:13:19,850
And then I can do
the optimizer step.

266
00:13:19,850 --> 00:13:24,670
And then I all-gather
the parameters.

267
00:13:24,670 --> 00:13:28,070
The second thing is
for the gradients,

268
00:13:28,070 --> 00:13:29,770
I didn't use all of them, right?

269
00:13:29,770 --> 00:13:31,830
So when I did the
reduce scatter,

270
00:13:31,830 --> 00:13:39,870
I only used the shards
that I optimized later.

271
00:13:39,870 --> 00:13:43,390
So I can just throw
the parts that I

272
00:13:43,390 --> 00:13:44,870
don't need from the gradients.

273
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:46,970
And this is ZeRO 2.

274
00:13:46,970 --> 00:13:51,170
So ZeRO 2 optimizes or
saves memory even further

275
00:13:51,170 --> 00:13:54,542
by just discarding the
gradients that I don't use.

276
00:13:54,542 --> 00:13:56,750
And this is the difference
between ZeRO 1 and ZeRO 2.

277
00:13:56,750 --> 00:13:58,030
It's a small difference.

278
00:13:58,030 --> 00:14:01,510
It's just that I don't
need to keep the gradients.

279
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:03,130
So it's easier said than done.

280
00:14:03,130 --> 00:14:05,450
Because in the
implementation, it's

281
00:14:05,450 --> 00:14:08,150
annoying to just discard things.

282
00:14:08,150 --> 00:14:13,450
So people usually just use ZeRO
1 and they keep the gradients.

283
00:14:13,450 --> 00:14:20,330
And also an important note since
now there is Muon optimizer,

284
00:14:20,330 --> 00:14:24,130
optimizer requires the
full tensors grads.

285
00:14:24,130 --> 00:14:28,370
So previously, people would
take their entire parameters,

286
00:14:28,370 --> 00:14:30,030
and they would just shard them.

287
00:14:30,030 --> 00:14:31,710
They would flatten everything.

288
00:14:31,710 --> 00:14:34,170
So we don't keep the
notion of a tensor.

289
00:14:34,170 --> 00:14:36,730
You just flatten
all your parameters,

290
00:14:36,730 --> 00:14:41,090
and then you would shard
them along the number of GPUs

291
00:14:41,090 --> 00:14:41,790
you have.

292
00:14:41,790 --> 00:14:46,030
But the problem is for Muon, you
can just split your parameters.

293
00:14:46,030 --> 00:14:52,030
You want your tensors to be full
so that you can do the Newton

294
00:14:52,030 --> 00:14:53,770
optimization on them.

295
00:14:53,770 --> 00:14:56,910
So the thing is
here, when you want

296
00:14:56,910 --> 00:15:01,010
to split your parameters or
your gradients on your GPUs,

297
00:15:01,010 --> 00:15:04,770
you need to make sure to
keep your tensors full.

298
00:15:04,770 --> 00:15:08,090
So for example, I'm going to
give the first, I don't know,

299
00:15:08,090 --> 00:15:12,950
five full tensors to the
first GPU and the five--

300
00:15:12,950 --> 00:15:16,750
the second five tensors
to the second GPU.

301
00:15:16,750 --> 00:15:20,950
So this is for Muon, but
also for other optimizers.

302
00:15:20,950 --> 00:15:23,790
And lastly, for
ZeRO 3, I also want

303
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:27,110
to save memory of
the parameters.

304
00:15:27,110 --> 00:15:32,430
And this is the craziest part,
because how can I save memory

305
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:36,710
by sharding the
model's memory when

306
00:15:36,710 --> 00:15:39,190
I need them in the
forward and the backward?

307
00:15:39,190 --> 00:15:45,180
And there is a very nice trick
that the ZeRO 3 engineers came

308
00:15:45,180 --> 00:15:50,420
from-- came with is when
I do the optimizer step,

309
00:15:50,420 --> 00:15:53,620
I don't need to all
gather right away.

310
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:59,900
I can just keep the shards
of the model on each GPU,

311
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:04,020
and whenever I need to do
the forward of that layer,

312
00:16:04,020 --> 00:16:05,900
for example, for
the first layer,

313
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:09,980
I'm just going to ask
for these other shards

314
00:16:09,980 --> 00:16:11,400
from the other GPUs.

315
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:13,660
So for example, for
the first layer,

316
00:16:13,660 --> 00:16:16,240
I'm going to ask for
the other shards.

317
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:17,560
I'm going to do the forward.

318
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:20,080
And then I'm going to
free the other parameters.

319
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:23,700
And then for the other layer,
I'm going to do the same thing.

320
00:16:23,700 --> 00:16:26,640
So this looks very slow, right?

321
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:31,240
Because on each layer, you
need to ask for all the--

322
00:16:31,240 --> 00:16:34,860
for the rest of your parameters
from your other GPUs,

323
00:16:34,860 --> 00:16:37,140
do the forward and
then free them.

324
00:16:37,140 --> 00:16:40,260
But in practice,
it works very well.

325
00:16:40,260 --> 00:16:43,520
And the reason is--

326
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:46,280
by the way, it's the
same for the backward.

327
00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:48,560
So for the backward,
we also just do

328
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:52,760
the all-gather of the weights
and then compute the backward

329
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:56,280
and we free the weights.

330
00:16:56,280 --> 00:17:03,160
So the reason is we
prefetch the next layer

331
00:17:03,160 --> 00:17:05,640
while computing
the current layer.

332
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:08,000
So this is the trick.

333
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,640
So I had previously
an all-gather

334
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:13,200
here after the optimizer step.

335
00:17:13,200 --> 00:17:18,378
But now, I just separated the
all-gather on multiple layers.

336
00:17:18,378 --> 00:17:19,920
And for example,
for the first layer,

337
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:21,339
I'm going to all-gather it here.

338
00:17:21,339 --> 00:17:22,780
I'm going to do the compute.

339
00:17:22,780 --> 00:17:26,599
And while I'm computing for
the forward of the first layer,

340
00:17:26,599 --> 00:17:32,840
I'm already all-gathering
the next layer, et cetera.

341
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:34,280
And then I free it, et cetera.

342
00:17:34,280 --> 00:17:40,740
And so concretely, I
never have more than two

343
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:45,040
what we call FSDP modules
or FSDP units in my memory,

344
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:50,220
because I always free one when
I all-gather the other one.

345
00:17:50,220 --> 00:17:52,900
And the same thing
for the backward.

346
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:56,740
And so I still do the
distributed optimizer and I

347
00:17:56,740 --> 00:17:57,560
repeat.

348
00:17:57,560 --> 00:18:01,520
So this is basically the best
form of DP that we can have.

349
00:18:01,520 --> 00:18:02,020
Why?

350
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:05,680
Because I efficiently
optimize my memory.

351
00:18:05,680 --> 00:18:08,720
I saved memory on
optimizer states,

352
00:18:08,720 --> 00:18:11,460
on gradients, and on the model.

353
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:14,080
And I also prefetch.

354
00:18:14,080 --> 00:18:16,560
So everything is
very well-overlapped.

355
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:19,920
And besides that, similar
to DP's bucket size,

356
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:25,820
I can also control the
size of the buckets, which

357
00:18:25,820 --> 00:18:31,380
we call the size of the
FSDP modules that we have.

358
00:18:31,380 --> 00:18:34,300
And so for the code-- so
there is two-- by the way,

359
00:18:34,300 --> 00:18:36,200
ZeRO 3 is also called FSDP.

360
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:39,690
FSDP is fully sharded
data parallel.

361
00:18:39,690 --> 00:18:42,530
And there is two
versions in PyTorch.

362
00:18:42,530 --> 00:18:47,010
So FSDP1, we used to
just have a class FSDP

363
00:18:47,010 --> 00:18:49,410
where we can wrap
our model and we

364
00:18:49,410 --> 00:18:52,650
can define the
wrap policy, which

365
00:18:52,650 --> 00:18:54,850
is similar to the bucket size.

366
00:18:54,850 --> 00:18:59,170
And for FSDP2, there is now a
function called fully shard.

367
00:18:59,170 --> 00:19:01,830
And you just call
it over your model

368
00:19:01,830 --> 00:19:04,250
and it's going to
automatically transform

369
00:19:04,250 --> 00:19:07,890
your modules to FSDP modules.

370
00:19:07,890 --> 00:19:11,690
So the difference between the
two versions, the first one,

371
00:19:11,690 --> 00:19:14,910
as I said, it flattens
all of your parameters

372
00:19:14,910 --> 00:19:18,230
and we already talked
of the problems of that.

373
00:19:18,230 --> 00:19:20,250
For example, Muon
and some optimizers,

374
00:19:20,250 --> 00:19:22,670
they need full tensors,
and this is bad.

375
00:19:22,670 --> 00:19:25,810
So ideally, when you
shard your parameters,

376
00:19:25,810 --> 00:19:30,210
you want to keep full
tensors on each GPU.

377
00:19:30,210 --> 00:19:33,350
So FSDP2 enables that.

378
00:19:33,350 --> 00:19:37,510
The second thing is
FSDP2 uses DTensor, which

379
00:19:37,510 --> 00:19:43,430
is a new utility in PyTorch.

380
00:19:43,430 --> 00:19:48,030
And it helps being combined
with other forms of parallelism

381
00:19:48,030 --> 00:19:49,050
as we're going to see.

382
00:19:49,050 --> 00:19:52,950

383
00:19:52,950 --> 00:19:57,470
And so to recap, please
compare ZeRO 2 with ZeRO 3.

384
00:19:57,470 --> 00:20:01,670
So in ZeRO 2, we had
a big all-gather here

385
00:20:01,670 --> 00:20:05,630
to all-gather the parameters
that we optimized.

386
00:20:05,630 --> 00:20:12,190
And in ZeRO 3, I just
divided this big all-gather

387
00:20:12,190 --> 00:20:17,070
into smaller all gathers of
the different FSDP modules.

388
00:20:17,070 --> 00:20:23,070
So it allows me to overlap this
all gather with the forwards.

389
00:20:23,070 --> 00:20:26,190
And I can control the
size of the FSDP modules.

390
00:20:26,190 --> 00:20:28,030
And it's also model
agnostic, because I

391
00:20:28,030 --> 00:20:30,750
don't care about the
architecture of my model.

392
00:20:30,750 --> 00:20:32,690
And it's just great.

393
00:20:32,690 --> 00:20:34,670
It works very good.

394
00:20:34,670 --> 00:20:38,430
So the pros and
cons of DP ZeRO 3.

395
00:20:38,430 --> 00:20:40,210
It creates memory for comms.

396
00:20:40,210 --> 00:20:44,350
So DP Vanilla, I didn't
optimize memory at all.

397
00:20:44,350 --> 00:20:47,430
I just assumed that the
model fits in memory.

398
00:20:47,430 --> 00:20:52,040
But if I needed to save memory,
I can just do ZeRO 1 or ZeRO 3

399
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:54,010
or ZeRO 2 or ZeRO 3.

400
00:20:54,010 --> 00:20:56,170
And this is something
to pay attention to.

401
00:20:56,170 --> 00:21:00,350
So a lot of people just throw
FSDP2 on all of their models.

402
00:21:00,350 --> 00:21:01,970
And this is not a good thing.

403
00:21:01,970 --> 00:21:06,850
Because if your model
fits on GPUs with ZeRO 1,

404
00:21:06,850 --> 00:21:09,650
you don't need ZeRO
3 because ZeRO 3

405
00:21:09,650 --> 00:21:12,050
you're going to add more
communications than you're going

406
00:21:12,050 --> 00:21:14,010
to make your training slower.

407
00:21:14,010 --> 00:21:18,130
So just use the ZeRO degree
that you need to save

408
00:21:18,130 --> 00:21:20,230
memory, because there
is no other benefits.

409
00:21:20,230 --> 00:21:25,410
If ZeRO 1 fits, it's going
to be faster than ZeRO 3.

410
00:21:25,410 --> 00:21:29,110
So the second thing is the great
overlap of comms with compute.

411
00:21:29,110 --> 00:21:31,690
As we've seen, everything
is well-overlapped.

412
00:21:31,690 --> 00:21:33,470
And the third thing
is model-agnostic.

413
00:21:33,470 --> 00:21:35,900
So there is no headache
of implementation.

414
00:21:35,900 --> 00:21:39,600
You can just-- whatever
the PyTorch module is,

415
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:41,600
you can just wrap it with FSDP.

416
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:43,420
You don't care if
it's a state model.

417
00:21:43,420 --> 00:21:45,180
You don't care if it's anything.

418
00:21:45,180 --> 00:21:48,080
It's going to work
out of the box.

419
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:52,240
The negatives, though,
is that at scale,

420
00:21:52,240 --> 00:22:00,440
since you need to all-gather the
parameters for each FSDP module,

421
00:22:00,440 --> 00:22:04,660
if you scale it too much,
even if it's overlapped,

422
00:22:04,660 --> 00:22:07,540
you can't hide the
communication anymore,

423
00:22:07,540 --> 00:22:09,260
because it's not perfect.

424
00:22:09,260 --> 00:22:15,200
If you have a very slow network,
you cannot overlap that anymore.

425
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:18,080
So there is a solution
for that, which is

426
00:22:18,080 --> 00:22:21,880
called hybrid sharding or HSDP.

427
00:22:21,880 --> 00:22:26,840
So you would use FSDP on a
slow-- on a smaller circle.

428
00:22:26,840 --> 00:22:31,320
And on the larger circle,
you can use Vanilla DP.

429
00:22:31,320 --> 00:22:34,540
So this is something
you can look up later.

430
00:22:34,540 --> 00:22:38,660
And the other thing
is ZeRO 3 is--

431
00:22:38,660 --> 00:22:40,580
we're still in data parallel.

432
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:43,640
So we're still
scaling with batches.

433
00:22:43,640 --> 00:22:46,360
So every GPU has
a different batch.

434
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:49,880
And we still have
this restriction.

435
00:22:49,880 --> 00:22:52,340
We can't scale indefinitely.

436
00:22:52,340 --> 00:22:57,960
So what if I reached
my global batch size?

437
00:22:57,960 --> 00:23:01,620
So I have, I don't
know, 10,000 GPUs.

438
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:06,120
And at 1,000 GPUs, I already
reach my global batch size.

439
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:09,100
I need another form
of parallelism.

440
00:23:09,100 --> 00:23:12,300
So let's take a look at TP.

441
00:23:12,300 --> 00:23:14,580
And the motivation
behind tensor parallelism

442
00:23:14,580 --> 00:23:19,380
is that if I have a model,
transformer model, for example,

443
00:23:19,380 --> 00:23:22,460
I want to keep the same
batch, so the same inputs,

444
00:23:22,460 --> 00:23:25,940
the same data, and
chart the model so

445
00:23:25,940 --> 00:23:29,660
that I do the same computation
as if I had one GPU.

446
00:23:29,660 --> 00:23:33,220
So basically, instead of doing
the computation with one GPU.

447
00:23:33,220 --> 00:23:36,240
I would do it with two.

448
00:23:36,240 --> 00:23:40,080
And essentially,
I'm going to shard

449
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:44,760
the memory for my model for
my gradients optimizer states

450
00:23:44,760 --> 00:23:46,540
by the number of GPUs I have.

451
00:23:46,540 --> 00:23:49,560

452
00:23:49,560 --> 00:23:55,040
So the question is-- and
this is a very fun question

453
00:23:55,040 --> 00:23:57,800
that people like to ask
in interviews, et cetera.

454
00:23:57,800 --> 00:24:02,160
So do we cope-- do we keep
full or half activations

455
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:03,460
in tensor parallel?

456
00:24:03,460 --> 00:24:06,120
And we're going to
answer that after a bit.

457
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:08,400
So let's start with
an easy example, which

458
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:10,700
is matrix multiplication.

459
00:24:10,700 --> 00:24:13,280
So if we take X
multiplied by Y--

460
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:18,200
by W here, a simple
approach, and we want

461
00:24:18,200 --> 00:24:20,320
to parallelize that onto GPUs.

462
00:24:20,320 --> 00:24:25,000
We can just split our
Matrix on columns.

463
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:31,040
And then I can
all-gather the outputs.

464
00:24:31,040 --> 00:24:34,310
Let's take the example
of two matmuls now.

465
00:24:34,310 --> 00:24:38,510
So I have Y1, which is X
multiplied YW1, and Y2, which

466
00:24:38,510 --> 00:24:40,270
is Y2 multiplied by W2.

467
00:24:40,270 --> 00:24:44,070
So if I split my first
weight by columns

468
00:24:44,070 --> 00:24:47,190
and if I split the
second one by rows,

469
00:24:47,190 --> 00:24:52,370
I can do the same computation,
which is two multiplications.

470
00:24:52,370 --> 00:24:55,510
And then I do an
AllReduce here, and I

471
00:24:55,510 --> 00:24:59,550
would get the same result
and the same computation

472
00:24:59,550 --> 00:25:02,510
as if I did this with one GPU.

473
00:25:02,510 --> 00:25:06,170
So this is the power
of tensor parallelism,

474
00:25:06,170 --> 00:25:09,430
and this is the
intuition behind it.

475
00:25:09,430 --> 00:25:13,830
If I had one GPU, I would
do two matrix multiplication

476
00:25:13,830 --> 00:25:16,430
with the full matrices.

477
00:25:16,430 --> 00:25:23,110
So I managed to distribute
this operation on two GPUs

478
00:25:23,110 --> 00:25:26,110
by splitting the weights.

479
00:25:26,110 --> 00:25:29,310
So each GPU only
has half the weights

480
00:25:29,310 --> 00:25:34,570
and each GPU only
does half the compute.

481
00:25:34,570 --> 00:25:37,810
But the caveat of
this is that I need

482
00:25:37,810 --> 00:25:42,010
to do a distributed
communication on AllReduce

483
00:25:42,010 --> 00:25:44,930
to keep the correctness.

484
00:25:44,930 --> 00:25:49,370
How does this work for
the backward, though?

485
00:25:49,370 --> 00:25:51,450
It's the same thing.

486
00:25:51,450 --> 00:25:53,170
And again, I'm going
to assume that I

487
00:25:53,170 --> 00:25:57,510
have the same upstream grad,
and this is a big headache.

488
00:25:57,510 --> 00:25:59,450
We can talk about it in a bit.

489
00:25:59,450 --> 00:26:03,850
So if we assume that I have
the same upstream grad, which

490
00:26:03,850 --> 00:26:07,650
is this, the Y2 and
Y2, similarly, it's

491
00:26:07,650 --> 00:26:11,990
just going to be multiplied
by the transpose of W2 and W1.

492
00:26:11,990 --> 00:26:15,330
This is, of course, to
compute the dx, which

493
00:26:15,330 --> 00:26:19,250
is the Dout, the out gradient.

494
00:26:19,250 --> 00:26:20,770
So again, it's just--

495
00:26:20,770 --> 00:26:23,430
I'm going to do
two multiplication.

496
00:26:23,430 --> 00:26:26,250
And again, it's just
half the weights.

497
00:26:26,250 --> 00:26:29,470
And again, I just need
to do an AllReduce.

498
00:26:29,470 --> 00:26:35,270
So to efficiently distribute
two matrix multiplications

499
00:26:35,270 --> 00:26:37,990
on multiple GPUs,
I'm going to need

500
00:26:37,990 --> 00:26:41,390
one AllReduce in the
forward and one AllReduce

501
00:26:41,390 --> 00:26:44,910
in the backward
under the assumption

502
00:26:44,910 --> 00:26:51,070
that I have the same upstream
gradients, which is the outputs,

503
00:26:51,070 --> 00:26:54,510
and I had the same inputs.

504
00:26:54,510 --> 00:26:57,510
So let's apply this on our MLP.

505
00:26:57,510 --> 00:27:03,230
So in MLP, we have, for example,
Y activation of X times A.

506
00:27:03,230 --> 00:27:06,050
And then I multiply
it by another matrix.

507
00:27:06,050 --> 00:27:09,830
So I always going to have
two matrix multiplications.

508
00:27:09,830 --> 00:27:11,490
So I apply the same trick.

509
00:27:11,490 --> 00:27:13,730
The first one is going
to be a column linear.

510
00:27:13,730 --> 00:27:15,550
The second one is a row linear.

511
00:27:15,550 --> 00:27:18,930
And I need an AllReduce
to keep the math correct.

512
00:27:18,930 --> 00:27:23,230
And as we said, we assume--
and this is a big assumption.

513
00:27:23,230 --> 00:27:26,070
In our transformer,
we assume that I'm

514
00:27:26,070 --> 00:27:29,760
going to always have the same
activations, the same inputs,

515
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:34,780
the same data, and I need to
have the same output and later

516
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:36,760
the same upstream gradients.

517
00:27:36,760 --> 00:27:39,900
So all the operations
after that must

518
00:27:39,900 --> 00:27:42,760
be the same in the two GPUs.

519
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:45,580

520
00:27:45,580 --> 00:27:48,760
And another thing to
basically what we said,

521
00:27:48,760 --> 00:27:52,160
we're going to divide
the compute by half.

522
00:27:52,160 --> 00:27:58,340
So A1 and A2 are halves of
the original matrix A, right?

523
00:27:58,340 --> 00:28:01,200
So hidden_dim is
sharded in here.

524
00:28:01,200 --> 00:28:04,180

525
00:28:04,180 --> 00:28:05,920
And this is what we just said.

526
00:28:05,920 --> 00:28:07,880
We need the same input.

527
00:28:07,880 --> 00:28:11,020
And we need the same
upstream gradient

528
00:28:11,020 --> 00:28:14,580
to keep the math correct.

529
00:28:14,580 --> 00:28:16,580
Let's take an
example of attention.

530
00:28:16,580 --> 00:28:19,480
So for attention, similarly, we
have two matrix multiplication,

531
00:28:19,480 --> 00:28:24,740
which is the QKV projections
and the output projections.

532
00:28:24,740 --> 00:28:29,240
So the question would
be should we shard along

533
00:28:29,240 --> 00:28:33,280
a hidden dimension or
number of heads or both.

534
00:28:33,280 --> 00:28:36,120
And what does it mean?

535
00:28:36,120 --> 00:28:38,680
So the question you
really want to ask--

536
00:28:38,680 --> 00:28:40,700
and this is a very
generic question.

537
00:28:40,700 --> 00:28:42,600
If you have a
model and you don't

538
00:28:42,600 --> 00:28:45,940
know how to implement
tensor parallelism on it,

539
00:28:45,940 --> 00:28:49,180
or you want to check that the
implementation is correct,

540
00:28:49,180 --> 00:28:51,520
you should really
ask, would GPUs

541
00:28:51,520 --> 00:28:55,600
compute the same
activations as without TP?

542
00:28:55,600 --> 00:29:00,360
So let's say I sharded
a long head dimension.

543
00:29:00,360 --> 00:29:04,120
Then when I do the softmax,
which is Q times K,

544
00:29:04,120 --> 00:29:06,460
it's going to have a
reduced hidden dimension.

545
00:29:06,460 --> 00:29:09,340
And that would impact
the softmax calculation.

546
00:29:09,340 --> 00:29:16,200
So it wouldn't give the same
results as if I had a TP one.

547
00:29:16,200 --> 00:29:19,260
But if I sharded a
long number of heads,

548
00:29:19,260 --> 00:29:23,160
since the heads
are independent, I

549
00:29:23,160 --> 00:29:30,500
would get the same activations
as if I didn't use TP.

550
00:29:30,500 --> 00:29:32,820
So this is really
the whole thing.

551
00:29:32,820 --> 00:29:36,340
So we showed a long
number of heads

552
00:29:36,340 --> 00:29:43,560
since the attention heads are
independent to keep attention

553
00:29:43,560 --> 00:29:45,060
correct.

554
00:29:45,060 --> 00:29:48,460
And again, we need
the same inputs,

555
00:29:48,460 --> 00:29:51,500
and we need the same
upstream gradients.

556
00:29:51,500 --> 00:29:55,340
So as a recap, the pros
is that we shard the model

557
00:29:55,340 --> 00:29:58,060
and compute across
GPUs and we're

558
00:29:58,060 --> 00:30:00,160
memory and sample efficient.

559
00:30:00,160 --> 00:30:03,560
So at the same time,
I sharded memory.

560
00:30:03,560 --> 00:30:07,780
So model parameters gradients
and optimizer states.

561
00:30:07,780 --> 00:30:11,760
And I didn't need to
have multiple batches.

562
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:16,380
So I used the same
sample across my GPUs.

563
00:30:16,380 --> 00:30:21,580
The second thing I didn't need
an AllReduce for the gradients

564
00:30:21,580 --> 00:30:25,270
because all GPUs I'm assuming
they're doing the same work.

565
00:30:25,270 --> 00:30:28,210
It's like I have
one big GPU that I

566
00:30:28,210 --> 00:30:31,930
distributed on smaller GPUs.

567
00:30:31,930 --> 00:30:34,970
And the cons is that
communication is heavy

568
00:30:34,970 --> 00:30:38,470
because at each attention,
at each MLP block,

569
00:30:38,470 --> 00:30:41,170
I need to do an
AllReduce for the forward

570
00:30:41,170 --> 00:30:43,250
and for the backward.

571
00:30:43,250 --> 00:30:48,590
The second thing is-- and this
is something we can see later.

572
00:30:48,590 --> 00:30:51,990
The parameters that are
outside the TP region,

573
00:30:51,990 --> 00:30:55,250
which is the region where the
hidden dimension is sharded,

574
00:30:55,250 --> 00:30:57,490
should stay synchronized.

575
00:30:57,490 --> 00:31:01,610
Otherwise, I won't
have the same inputs

576
00:31:01,610 --> 00:31:04,690
and the same upstream
gradients because this

577
00:31:04,690 --> 00:31:08,970
is a big assumption for the
matmuls to stay correct.

578
00:31:08,970 --> 00:31:14,730
So in practice, the parameters
that are outside the TP region,

579
00:31:14,730 --> 00:31:17,310
they need to stay the same.

580
00:31:17,310 --> 00:31:19,310
They need to stay duplicated.

581
00:31:19,310 --> 00:31:22,870
So in practice, we all
reduce their grads.

582
00:31:22,870 --> 00:31:25,750
And this is something
that screws up training

583
00:31:25,750 --> 00:31:29,070
when you use TP.

584
00:31:29,070 --> 00:31:31,450
And just due to
numerical precision--

585
00:31:31,450 --> 00:31:34,290
because theoretically,
you don't need to do this,

586
00:31:34,290 --> 00:31:38,850
but due to numerical
imprecisions, they drift apart.

587
00:31:38,850 --> 00:31:41,470
So in practice, we
always use their grads.

588
00:31:41,470 --> 00:31:44,670
And the third thing is, of
course, complex implementation.

589
00:31:44,670 --> 00:31:48,090
And it's not easy if you
change the architecture.

590
00:31:48,090 --> 00:31:54,750
Let's say you want to use MLA
or some other fancy attention.

591
00:31:54,750 --> 00:32:00,830
It's a headache to apply
tensor parallelism on it.

592
00:32:00,830 --> 00:32:03,590
So let's take a look at
sequence parallelism.

593
00:32:03,590 --> 00:32:06,790
So what's the motivation
behind sequence parallelism,

594
00:32:06,790 --> 00:32:10,650
which is a flavor to TP?

595
00:32:10,650 --> 00:32:14,510
So in TP, we've
seen that we have

596
00:32:14,510 --> 00:32:16,690
this TP region, which
is the attention,

597
00:32:16,690 --> 00:32:19,230
and this TP region,
which is the MLP.

598
00:32:19,230 --> 00:32:22,100
And we do the AllReduce here.

599
00:32:22,100 --> 00:32:24,980
So the second layers,
which are the row layers,

600
00:32:24,980 --> 00:32:26,240
they have an AllReduce.

601
00:32:26,240 --> 00:32:27,060
OK, cool.

602
00:32:27,060 --> 00:32:31,060
So in the TP region, the
shape is a sequence patch

603
00:32:31,060 --> 00:32:34,460
and we shard the hidden
dimension we set.

604
00:32:34,460 --> 00:32:39,940
And after the second
matrix in each TP region,

605
00:32:39,940 --> 00:32:42,280
I'm going to get back
the original shapes.

606
00:32:42,280 --> 00:32:46,020
So I'm going to get back the
original hidden dimension.

607
00:32:46,020 --> 00:32:51,860
So the question is these parts
are duplicated across GPUs

608
00:32:51,860 --> 00:32:55,320
and it's annoying, as we said,
to keep them synchronized.

609
00:32:55,320 --> 00:32:59,680
As we said in practice, we need
to always use this layer norm.

610
00:32:59,680 --> 00:33:01,400
And if I had some
other operation,

611
00:33:01,400 --> 00:33:06,500
I also need to make
sure it's replicated.

612
00:33:06,500 --> 00:33:09,480
So can we just
distribute them as well?

613
00:33:09,480 --> 00:33:14,040
And this is what sequence
parallelism tries to solve.

614
00:33:14,040 --> 00:33:17,060
And we're going to use the trick
that we talked about earlier.

615
00:33:17,060 --> 00:33:18,980
So we have an AllReduce.

616
00:33:18,980 --> 00:33:23,660
And we know that AllReduce, its
reduce-scatter and all-gather.

617
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:28,120
So how can we apply
it in this case?

618
00:33:28,120 --> 00:33:30,440
This is what sequence
parallelism is.

619
00:33:30,440 --> 00:33:33,600
So this AllReduce, I'm
going to replace it

620
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:36,400
with the reduce-scatter.

621
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:38,200
And I'm going to--

622
00:33:38,200 --> 00:33:41,100
this identity-- we can
call this an identity.

623
00:33:41,100 --> 00:33:44,160
This identity, I'm going to
transform it to all-gather.

624
00:33:44,160 --> 00:33:50,600
So in the TPU region, I'm
going to shard my activations

625
00:33:50,600 --> 00:33:52,840
along the hidden dimension.

626
00:33:52,840 --> 00:33:56,580
And then I reduce scatter
along the sequence dimension.

627
00:33:56,580 --> 00:34:01,480
So in this sequence
region, SP region,

628
00:34:01,480 --> 00:34:04,840
I have my sequence,
which is sharded by TP.

629
00:34:04,840 --> 00:34:14,600
And then I all-gather it again
to have H, which is sharded.

630
00:34:14,600 --> 00:34:18,040
So basically, I play
between sharding

631
00:34:18,040 --> 00:34:21,800
along the hidden dimension and
along the sequence dimension.

632
00:34:21,800 --> 00:34:26,100
So this is moving
from TP to TP with SP.

633
00:34:26,100 --> 00:34:30,639
And what this helps me
do is multiple things.

634
00:34:30,639 --> 00:34:33,639
So first of all, the
activations that are stored.

635
00:34:33,639 --> 00:34:36,639
So before, I had to store
very big activations here,

636
00:34:36,639 --> 00:34:39,980
which are SBH, but
thanks to sequence

637
00:34:39,980 --> 00:34:45,060
parallelism my size is
always divided by TP.

638
00:34:45,060 --> 00:34:50,260
So this helps me
avoid big activations.

639
00:34:50,260 --> 00:34:56,139
The second thing is it's the
same amount of communications

640
00:34:56,139 --> 00:34:59,340
since AllReduce,
as we've seen, it's

641
00:34:59,340 --> 00:35:01,420
just the reduce-scatter
and all-gather.

642
00:35:01,420 --> 00:35:05,340
So I didn't have to
add any communication.

643
00:35:05,340 --> 00:35:07,740
And the last question
is about layer norm.

644
00:35:07,740 --> 00:35:14,660
So since we did this operation,
should we think layer norms

645
00:35:14,660 --> 00:35:16,980
grids in this case?

646
00:35:16,980 --> 00:35:20,490
And the answer is no.

647
00:35:20,490 --> 00:35:24,810
And for this, let's take
a look at the backward.

648
00:35:24,810 --> 00:35:28,610
So similarly to what we did
before with the matmuls,

649
00:35:28,610 --> 00:35:33,530
you can verify later that the
backward of a reduce scatter

650
00:35:33,530 --> 00:35:36,810
is all gather and the
backward of an all gather

651
00:35:36,810 --> 00:35:38,010
is reduce-scatter.

652
00:35:38,010 --> 00:35:40,710
Basically reduce-scatter
and all-gather,

653
00:35:40,710 --> 00:35:44,450
they are inverse of each other.

654
00:35:44,450 --> 00:35:48,690
So if this is the forward, the
backward becomes like this.

655
00:35:48,690 --> 00:35:51,690
So the backward is I'm
going to do all gather here

656
00:35:51,690 --> 00:35:53,610
and then reduce
scatter here cetera.

657
00:35:53,610 --> 00:35:56,810
So we notice here that
before layer norms

658
00:35:56,810 --> 00:35:59,050
we have reduced scatter.

659
00:35:59,050 --> 00:36:05,090
And thanks to these reduced
scatter that I now added they

660
00:36:05,090 --> 00:36:08,110
take care of synchronizing
the gradients.

661
00:36:08,110 --> 00:36:11,950
So it's really beautiful
that it works out like this.

662
00:36:11,950 --> 00:36:16,510
So thanks to the fact
that now the GPUs,

663
00:36:16,510 --> 00:36:19,730
they don't do the same work,
they see different batches.

664
00:36:19,730 --> 00:36:22,230
And thanks to the fact
that in the backward

665
00:36:22,230 --> 00:36:25,110
I have a reduce
scatter here now,

666
00:36:25,110 --> 00:36:28,830
instead of just assuming
the perfect case

667
00:36:28,830 --> 00:36:32,270
that it's an identity and that
the layer norm would always

668
00:36:32,270 --> 00:36:34,630
stay synchronized.

669
00:36:34,630 --> 00:36:37,370
I just have this all-gather
and reduce-scatter,

670
00:36:37,370 --> 00:36:42,590
and they take care of
synchronizing my gradients.

671
00:36:42,590 --> 00:36:49,390
So you can verify this,
the inverse reduce-scatter

672
00:36:49,390 --> 00:36:51,310
and all-gather later.

673
00:36:51,310 --> 00:36:54,550
And the pros, cons,
so let's take a recap.

674
00:36:54,550 --> 00:36:57,710
So the pros is that now
we're sharding the model

675
00:36:57,710 --> 00:37:00,110
and compute across GPUs.

676
00:37:00,110 --> 00:37:02,750
And we're being memory
and sample efficient

677
00:37:02,750 --> 00:37:04,470
especially sample
efficient, which

678
00:37:04,470 --> 00:37:07,150
is what we didn't have in DP.

679
00:37:07,150 --> 00:37:12,030
And again, we don't
need grad AllReduce

680
00:37:12,030 --> 00:37:14,290
because GPUs are essentially
doing the same work.

681
00:37:14,290 --> 00:37:18,210
And thanks to SP, I don't
need to AllReduce layer norms

682
00:37:18,210 --> 00:37:21,290
as well because they now
have the reduce-scatter

683
00:37:21,290 --> 00:37:23,050
in the backward.

684
00:37:23,050 --> 00:37:24,990
But we still have
the same problem.

685
00:37:24,990 --> 00:37:29,970
We're communication heavy and
the implementation is complex.

686
00:37:29,970 --> 00:37:32,690
And for this
communication heavy,

687
00:37:32,690 --> 00:37:34,810
this is why it's
recommended if you

688
00:37:34,810 --> 00:37:38,690
take a look at the different
distributed training libraries.

689
00:37:38,690 --> 00:37:42,590
They recommend that you use
TP within a single node.

690
00:37:42,590 --> 00:37:45,450
So if you have a
node of 8 GPUs, they

691
00:37:45,450 --> 00:37:49,770
say that it's better
to use TP lower than 8

692
00:37:49,770 --> 00:37:53,370
so that the communications
for TP stay within a node,

693
00:37:53,370 --> 00:37:56,270
so that when you do
forwards and backwards,

694
00:37:56,270 --> 00:38:00,370
you don't get stuck
waiting for the AllReduces

695
00:38:00,370 --> 00:38:03,130
or the reduce-scatter and
all-gather in forwards

696
00:38:03,130 --> 00:38:04,930
and backwards.

697
00:38:04,930 --> 00:38:09,370
So now, we've seen two
axes of parallelism.

698
00:38:09,370 --> 00:38:12,970
And before generalizing
to the others,

699
00:38:12,970 --> 00:38:16,020
let's see what it
means to combine them.

700
00:38:16,020 --> 00:38:21,040
So we've seen the data
parallel, which shards data.

701
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:24,040
Here, we have data
parallel size three.

702
00:38:24,040 --> 00:38:27,520
And we've seen tensor
parallelism, which shards

703
00:38:27,520 --> 00:38:34,080
the model into dimensions.

704
00:38:34,080 --> 00:38:36,680
So what does it mean
to combine these two?

705
00:38:36,680 --> 00:38:39,240
Well, the cool thing
about the forms of

706
00:38:39,240 --> 00:38:41,360
parallelism that
we've seen that they

707
00:38:41,360 --> 00:38:43,100
are orthogonal of each other.

708
00:38:43,100 --> 00:38:50,420
So if batch is sharded along
the data axis, it's replicated.

709
00:38:50,420 --> 00:38:54,660
So it's replicated here along
the model axis and vice versa.

710
00:38:54,660 --> 00:38:59,480
If the model is sharded
along the TP axis,

711
00:38:59,480 --> 00:39:02,360
it's replicated
along the data axis.

712
00:39:02,360 --> 00:39:08,540
So this helps us make a
mesh of all the parallelism.

713
00:39:08,540 --> 00:39:11,840
Now, we've only seen two,
but later we can see five

714
00:39:11,840 --> 00:39:13,620
or there's even six or seven.

715
00:39:13,620 --> 00:39:17,380
It depends on how many
partners you want.

716
00:39:17,380 --> 00:39:21,340
And in the code, if you look
at TorchTitan or megatron

717
00:39:21,340 --> 00:39:24,700
or narratron, you're
going to see that--

718
00:39:24,700 --> 00:39:27,360
at first, they init
the process group,

719
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:29,000
so they init all the WORLD_SIZE.

720
00:39:29,000 --> 00:39:32,660
Let's say you have
six GPUs here.

721
00:39:32,660 --> 00:39:36,940
So you're going to create your
TP groups and your DP groups.

722
00:39:36,940 --> 00:39:39,820
And later, you can
just pick on which

723
00:39:39,820 --> 00:39:43,720
axis you want to do the
communication operations.

724
00:39:43,720 --> 00:39:48,120
So for example, I want to
already use along the TP group.

725
00:39:48,120 --> 00:39:50,540
So I'm going to AllReduce
like this and AllReduce

726
00:39:50,540 --> 00:39:53,960
like this along
all the other axes.

727
00:39:53,960 --> 00:39:57,920
So this is the beauty
of this parallelism.

728
00:39:57,920 --> 00:40:01,980
We make sure that they are
orthogonal of each other.

729
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:08,060
And now, we've gone through
the first two big parallelisms,

730
00:40:08,060 --> 00:40:12,140
the last three I'm going
to go fast over them.

731
00:40:12,140 --> 00:40:16,560
So for PP, it's
much easier than TP.

732
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:20,040
So we have our layers like this.

733
00:40:20,040 --> 00:40:24,002
And instead of sharding
them horizontally,

734
00:40:24,002 --> 00:40:25,460
I'm going to share
them vertically.

735
00:40:25,460 --> 00:40:28,120
So I'm going to put some
layers on the first GPU,

736
00:40:28,120 --> 00:40:29,700
some layers on the second GPU.

737
00:40:29,700 --> 00:40:33,660
But you can see here that
the problem is for GPU 1,

738
00:40:33,660 --> 00:40:36,380
at the beginning, it doesn't
have any activations.

739
00:40:36,380 --> 00:40:40,120
So it's just going to say
keep waiting for the first GPU

740
00:40:40,120 --> 00:40:45,012
and for the first
GPU after it goes--

741
00:40:45,012 --> 00:40:47,220
after the forward goes
through the first four layers,

742
00:40:47,220 --> 00:40:50,080
it just waits for the other GPU.

743
00:40:50,080 --> 00:40:53,240
So how can we solve this?

744
00:40:53,240 --> 00:40:55,320
And for pipeline
parallelism, there

745
00:40:55,320 --> 00:40:59,740
is what we call PP schedules
pipeline parallelism schedulers.

746
00:40:59,740 --> 00:41:03,400
So the schedulers,
they define how

747
00:41:03,400 --> 00:41:07,720
your GPUs are going to do--
are going to load the data,

748
00:41:07,720 --> 00:41:10,960
and they're going to communicate
activations and gradients

749
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:12,310
with the other ranks.

750
00:41:12,310 --> 00:41:15,270
So let's start with
a simple assumption.

751
00:41:15,270 --> 00:41:18,750
Each GPU has one
layer of your model.

752
00:41:18,750 --> 00:41:22,070
And we're going to start
with the simplest scheduler,

753
00:41:22,070 --> 00:41:26,110
the all forward, all
backward scheduler.

754
00:41:26,110 --> 00:41:29,850
So we're going to have the
first batch, which is this one.

755
00:41:29,850 --> 00:41:31,830
So it starts at the first GPU.

756
00:41:31,830 --> 00:41:33,990
And then he sends
the activations

757
00:41:33,990 --> 00:41:38,110
to the second GPU and then
third and then fourth.

758
00:41:38,110 --> 00:41:41,390
And then I have another batch.

759
00:41:41,390 --> 00:41:43,710
So the GPUs don't wait.

760
00:41:43,710 --> 00:41:46,470
I'm going to schedule
eight batches.

761
00:41:46,470 --> 00:41:48,830
So all of them, they're
going to do the forwards.

762
00:41:48,830 --> 00:41:51,690
And then they're going
to do the backwards.

763
00:41:51,690 --> 00:41:53,710
So every time one GPU
does the backward,

764
00:41:53,710 --> 00:41:57,570
it sends the gradients to the
previous layer, et cetera.

765
00:41:57,570 --> 00:41:58,930
And I repeat this.

766
00:41:58,930 --> 00:42:00,830
So this is all
forward all backward.

767
00:42:00,830 --> 00:42:07,190
And I schedule multiple batches
to minimize the idle time.

768
00:42:07,190 --> 00:42:09,890
So the idle time is this.

769
00:42:09,890 --> 00:42:15,350
And it's the biggest problem
in pipeline parallelism.

770
00:42:15,350 --> 00:42:18,370

771
00:42:18,370 --> 00:42:21,210
So there is-- the
best way to solve

772
00:42:21,210 --> 00:42:25,530
it is to have more
complex schedulers.

773
00:42:25,530 --> 00:42:30,170
So this is the one forward,
one backward scheduler.

774
00:42:30,170 --> 00:42:34,850
And the name comes from the fact
that if you prioritize backwards

775
00:42:34,850 --> 00:42:36,650
over forwards,
you're going to have

776
00:42:36,650 --> 00:42:38,750
at some point like
in the middle,

777
00:42:38,750 --> 00:42:41,110
you're going to have
a forward, backward.

778
00:42:41,110 --> 00:42:43,070
So they're interleaved
basically, forward,

779
00:42:43,070 --> 00:42:44,930
backward forward, backward.

780
00:42:44,930 --> 00:42:48,450
But we still didn't solve
the pipeline bubble.

781
00:42:48,450 --> 00:42:52,850
This just helps with
the memory a little bit.

782
00:42:52,850 --> 00:42:56,010
So the best way
to solve it would

783
00:42:56,010 --> 00:43:01,650
be to start doing forwards
from this side as well.

784
00:43:01,650 --> 00:43:08,490
And this is what Deepseek
has done in their DualPipe.

785
00:43:08,490 --> 00:43:13,790
So basically, if you
have eight devices,

786
00:43:13,790 --> 00:43:18,390
you're going to distribute the
layers in a round-robin fashion.

787
00:43:18,390 --> 00:43:22,230
So the first layer,
you're going to put it

788
00:43:22,230 --> 00:43:25,190
both on the first
device and last device.

789
00:43:25,190 --> 00:43:28,530
So that both of them, they can
start with data, et cetera.

790
00:43:28,530 --> 00:43:30,610
So there is a forward
that's going like this.

791
00:43:30,610 --> 00:43:32,648
And there's a forward
that's going like this.

792
00:43:32,648 --> 00:43:35,190
And then a backward like this,
and then a backward like this.

793
00:43:35,190 --> 00:43:38,370
And you manage the
overlap in the house.

794
00:43:38,370 --> 00:43:41,030
So as we said, it's
easier said than done

795
00:43:41,030 --> 00:43:44,250
because the implementation
is very complex.

796
00:43:44,250 --> 00:43:47,630
And you need to keep
track of the batches

797
00:43:47,630 --> 00:43:50,150
and the forwards
and backwards so

798
00:43:50,150 --> 00:43:53,630
that you send the correct
activations, and this--

799
00:43:53,630 --> 00:43:56,950
and the correct gradients.

800
00:43:56,950 --> 00:44:00,130
So in general,
the pros and cons.

801
00:44:00,130 --> 00:44:02,990
So the pros is that the
communications are cheap.

802
00:44:02,990 --> 00:44:07,490
There is no AllReduce, nothing
to have a headache about.

803
00:44:07,490 --> 00:44:11,140
You just exchange the
activations and the gradients.

804
00:44:11,140 --> 00:44:14,100
And the sharding
is very efficient

805
00:44:14,100 --> 00:44:19,420
because every GPU keeps track
of just a shard of the model.

806
00:44:19,420 --> 00:44:20,680
It's model-agnostic.

807
00:44:20,680 --> 00:44:23,200
I don't care about
the nature's model,

808
00:44:23,200 --> 00:44:26,020
as long as I just have
layers, and I just

809
00:44:26,020 --> 00:44:29,940
send activations and gradients.

810
00:44:29,940 --> 00:44:34,100
And the cons is that I
need to save activations

811
00:44:34,100 --> 00:44:37,280
for multiple micro-batches,
which is annoying.

812
00:44:37,280 --> 00:44:42,180
So for example, if in this case,
I had eight backwards in here,

813
00:44:42,180 --> 00:44:44,920
they scale 20 micro-batches.

814
00:44:44,920 --> 00:44:50,100
So I need to save 20 times the
activations for the different.

815
00:44:50,100 --> 00:44:57,500
So usually for PP and FSDP since
they rely on activations a lot,

816
00:44:57,500 --> 00:45:00,480
we offload the activations.

817
00:45:00,480 --> 00:45:02,460
So we use activation
checkpointing

818
00:45:02,460 --> 00:45:04,900
or now there is
even CPU offloading.

819
00:45:04,900 --> 00:45:09,000
If you have a good GPU, you
can offload the activations

820
00:45:09,000 --> 00:45:11,840
to the CPU, and then
you just load them back

821
00:45:11,840 --> 00:45:14,400
when you need them.

822
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:20,160
The other annoying thing is, if
you want to hide the PP bubble,

823
00:45:20,160 --> 00:45:24,480
you need an advanced pipeline
scheduler like dual pipe.

824
00:45:24,480 --> 00:45:26,780
And the implementation
is complex.

825
00:45:26,780 --> 00:45:31,280
It's very hard and it's
tough to add to the library.

826
00:45:31,280 --> 00:45:35,340
And 13 is you need multiple
micro-batches to hide PP bubble.

827
00:45:35,340 --> 00:45:38,440
So in a sense, this is
like the deep issue,

828
00:45:38,440 --> 00:45:41,880
is that you need to
scale your global batch

829
00:45:41,880 --> 00:45:45,120
size with the PP size.

830
00:45:45,120 --> 00:45:47,880
And the last two
parallelisms, they

831
00:45:47,880 --> 00:45:50,320
are specific to two use cases.

832
00:45:50,320 --> 00:45:54,080
For example, for CPP, the
issue we want to solve

833
00:45:54,080 --> 00:45:55,700
is sequence parallelism.

834
00:45:55,700 --> 00:46:01,440
So once you scale your sequence
length, the activations,

835
00:46:01,440 --> 00:46:03,480
they explode.

836
00:46:03,480 --> 00:46:05,620
So the question--
even with TP, even

837
00:46:05,620 --> 00:46:09,060
with PP, the question
is, how can we

838
00:46:09,060 --> 00:46:15,740
amortize the memory cost when
doing long contact training?

839
00:46:15,740 --> 00:46:18,180
So what we've seen previously
in data parallelism, when

840
00:46:18,180 --> 00:46:23,100
we have a batch, we can shard
it along the batch dimension.

841
00:46:23,100 --> 00:46:27,220
But the other-- what
we can do now is let's

842
00:46:27,220 --> 00:46:31,060
shard it along the
sequence dimension.

843
00:46:31,060 --> 00:46:35,020
So it's analogous
to data parallelism.

844
00:46:35,020 --> 00:46:39,540
But if I do that, and if I
give a different sequences

845
00:46:39,540 --> 00:46:42,360
to the GPUs, what
about attention?

846
00:46:42,360 --> 00:46:44,860
Because when I do
the forward, each GPU

847
00:46:44,860 --> 00:46:46,860
has only a part of the sequence.

848
00:46:46,860 --> 00:46:51,860
So how can I compute attention
over my entire sequence?

849
00:46:51,860 --> 00:46:54,860
This is where ring
attention comes from.

850
00:46:54,860 --> 00:46:58,420
And the idea is similar
to online softmax that

851
00:46:58,420 --> 00:47:00,980
is used in flash attention.

852
00:47:00,980 --> 00:47:04,440
So you don't need to compute
softmax out of the box.

853
00:47:04,440 --> 00:47:08,590
You can compute it
in an online fashion.

854
00:47:08,590 --> 00:47:14,250
So each GPU, they
compute softmax locally

855
00:47:14,250 --> 00:47:19,170
and then they exchange K
and V with the other GPUs.

856
00:47:19,170 --> 00:47:22,690
Then they update the
softmax, et cetera.

857
00:47:22,690 --> 00:47:26,690
So this is the idea
behind ring attention.

858
00:47:26,690 --> 00:47:29,130
So the problem with
this, the small problem,

859
00:47:29,130 --> 00:47:33,210
is that we need to communicate
K and V inside the attention

860
00:47:33,210 --> 00:47:33,710
block.

861
00:47:33,710 --> 00:47:36,850
So inside each
attention block, which

862
00:47:36,850 --> 00:47:43,410
is a similar problem to TP, so
it's also communication heavy.

863
00:47:43,410 --> 00:47:48,770
And for that, we can use send
receive operation for GPUs

864
00:47:48,770 --> 00:47:51,230
to send the other case.

865
00:47:51,230 --> 00:47:55,570
And this you can also
use all-gather for that.

866
00:47:55,570 --> 00:47:58,010
So the pros is it's
the only parallelism

867
00:47:58,010 --> 00:48:03,990
that efficiently partition
large sequences memory, sorry.

868
00:48:03,990 --> 00:48:05,810
And because the
other parallelisms,

869
00:48:05,810 --> 00:48:08,730
they don't really
shard the sequence.

870
00:48:08,730 --> 00:48:12,350
So if you have a big
sequence, there's a problem.

871
00:48:12,350 --> 00:48:14,750
The cons is that it's
communication heavy

872
00:48:14,750 --> 00:48:17,990
because in each attention
block, you need to exchange

873
00:48:17,990 --> 00:48:19,390
K's and V's.

874
00:48:19,390 --> 00:48:24,130
And similar to DP, since
each GPU sees different data,

875
00:48:24,130 --> 00:48:26,910
we need to AllReduce
the gradients

876
00:48:26,910 --> 00:48:30,630
and it doesn't help much
for short sequences.

877
00:48:30,630 --> 00:48:34,350
So really, it's really
used if and only

878
00:48:34,350 --> 00:48:39,030
if you're doing long
context training.

879
00:48:39,030 --> 00:48:44,310
And for the last one
EP, it's used for MoEs.

880
00:48:44,310 --> 00:48:49,270
And for MoEs, we don't have
time to go really through them.

881
00:48:49,270 --> 00:48:53,110
But how can we parallelize them?

882
00:48:53,110 --> 00:48:56,190
So when you have
your router and you

883
00:48:56,190 --> 00:49:00,510
want to route your tokens
to different experts,

884
00:49:00,510 --> 00:49:04,930
you can just shard your
experts across GPUs.

885
00:49:04,930 --> 00:49:08,690
So the question
would be, what comes

886
00:49:08,690 --> 00:49:13,450
would I add so that
I can do the routing?

887
00:49:13,450 --> 00:49:17,110
For routing, there is a switch
transformer and top K version.

888
00:49:17,110 --> 00:49:23,250
So let's talk about top K,
which is the more generic one.

889
00:49:23,250 --> 00:49:27,170
For this, let's take a look
at all-to-all operation.

890
00:49:27,170 --> 00:49:31,730
So all-to-all, it's
the most generic way

891
00:49:31,730 --> 00:49:33,470
of exchanging messages.

892
00:49:33,470 --> 00:49:35,050
So basically, you
have, for example,

893
00:49:35,050 --> 00:49:37,610
four processes, four GPUs.

894
00:49:37,610 --> 00:49:41,450
Each one of them, they have
four different messages.

895
00:49:41,450 --> 00:49:46,770
So all-to-all what it
does is just each process,

896
00:49:46,770 --> 00:49:51,410
it's going to send their
messages to the other four.

897
00:49:51,410 --> 00:49:57,290
And each process is going to get
messages from the other four.

898
00:49:57,290 --> 00:50:00,210
So in a sense, all
processes, they

899
00:50:00,210 --> 00:50:01,460
communicate with each other.

900
00:50:01,460 --> 00:50:04,460
It's like an n squared
type of communication.

901
00:50:04,460 --> 00:50:07,160
It's the more-- it's
the most complex one.

902
00:50:07,160 --> 00:50:10,600
And how does this apply in MoEs?

903
00:50:10,600 --> 00:50:15,520
So let's say each GPU
has different experts.

904
00:50:15,520 --> 00:50:19,040
So when you're
doing the routing,

905
00:50:19,040 --> 00:50:23,040
each GPU is going to have
some tokens that need

906
00:50:23,040 --> 00:50:25,920
to be routed to the first GPU.

907
00:50:25,920 --> 00:50:27,960
So this is why all-to-all helps.

908
00:50:27,960 --> 00:50:32,200
So the first GPU here,
which has the first expert,

909
00:50:32,200 --> 00:50:36,080
needs to get the
tokens associated

910
00:50:36,080 --> 00:50:38,620
to it from the other GPUs.

911
00:50:38,620 --> 00:50:42,040

912
00:50:42,040 --> 00:50:46,300
So how can we apply
expert parallelism?

913
00:50:46,300 --> 00:50:49,080
First, we shard the
experts across GPUs

914
00:50:49,080 --> 00:50:50,880
and only the experts.

915
00:50:50,880 --> 00:50:55,740
So expert parallelism is
really only applied in MoE.

916
00:50:55,740 --> 00:50:58,600
It doesn't touch attention.

917
00:50:58,600 --> 00:51:03,780
And so to avoid duplicated
work in attention,

918
00:51:03,780 --> 00:51:06,420
we need to share
data across GPUs.

919
00:51:06,420 --> 00:51:13,260
So EP is-- actually, it's not
orthogonal to DP in this sense.

920
00:51:13,260 --> 00:51:18,180
So you also shard data
across expert parallelism.

921
00:51:18,180 --> 00:51:21,740
And we need an all-to-all
communication to route tokens

922
00:51:21,740 --> 00:51:25,380
to their respective experts,
which is the operation

923
00:51:25,380 --> 00:51:26,820
that we call dispatch.

924
00:51:26,820 --> 00:51:32,700
And of course, we also need to
combine them after the experts

925
00:51:32,700 --> 00:51:36,580
to retrieve the
original sequences.

926
00:51:36,580 --> 00:51:40,140
So the problem and the
bigger-- the biggest problem

927
00:51:40,140 --> 00:51:43,300
that you're going to
face when scaling MoEs.

928
00:51:43,300 --> 00:51:50,360
So how do we know what
all-to-all dispatch to use?

929
00:51:50,360 --> 00:51:54,580
So basically, in order to know
how the tokens are routed,

930
00:51:54,580 --> 00:51:59,830
you need for the router
to compute the scores.

931
00:51:59,830 --> 00:52:05,050
So the CPU needs to
wait for the router

932
00:52:05,050 --> 00:52:08,690
to compute the expert scores.

933
00:52:08,690 --> 00:52:09,910
And this is annoying.

934
00:52:09,910 --> 00:52:10,410
Why?

935
00:52:10,410 --> 00:52:15,530
Because the CPU needs
to wait here for the GPU

936
00:52:15,530 --> 00:52:19,690
to do the router
calculation, what we

937
00:52:19,690 --> 00:52:21,650
call the dispatch preprocess.

938
00:52:21,650 --> 00:52:25,690
And the GPU needs to tell us
what are the buffer sizes,

939
00:52:25,690 --> 00:52:30,010
and what are the tokens
per expert that we need.

940
00:52:30,010 --> 00:52:33,850
And to solve this, there's, for
example, deep EP by Deepseek

941
00:52:33,850 --> 00:52:37,170
and there is hybrid
EP by NVIDIA.

942
00:52:37,170 --> 00:52:40,770
And the only way
to solve this is

943
00:52:40,770 --> 00:52:48,290
to use recent hardware so
far, which has IBGDI and RDMA.

944
00:52:48,290 --> 00:52:50,530
So these allow us to--

945
00:52:50,530 --> 00:52:57,270
so they allocate specific
SMs and specific memory

946
00:52:57,270 --> 00:53:00,190
to basically--

947
00:53:00,190 --> 00:53:04,710
whenever they
calculate or they know

948
00:53:04,710 --> 00:53:07,630
which tokens to
route to each GPU,

949
00:53:07,630 --> 00:53:14,350
they have already allocated the
memory for that calculation.

950
00:53:14,350 --> 00:53:16,290
And this is a problem
because for example,

951
00:53:16,290 --> 00:53:22,430
if you have H hundreds or if you
don't have InfiniBand network,

952
00:53:22,430 --> 00:53:24,910
you're stuck with
this CPU-GPU sink.

953
00:53:24,910 --> 00:53:29,750
And this is why most labs
who do MoE trainings,

954
00:53:29,750 --> 00:53:32,310
they have very slow
trainings just because

955
00:53:32,310 --> 00:53:34,870
of this problem of hardware.

956
00:53:34,870 --> 00:53:40,130
And even the Deep EP, that
Deepseek has open source,

957
00:53:40,130 --> 00:53:43,230
you're going to find
out that it only works

958
00:53:43,230 --> 00:53:46,410
on IBGDI, which is InfiniBand.

959
00:53:46,410 --> 00:53:52,190
So the other labs have a hard
time catching up with this.

960
00:53:52,190 --> 00:53:55,910
So in summary,
expert parallelism

961
00:53:55,910 --> 00:53:59,330
is the only way to
distribute MoEs efficiently,

962
00:53:59,330 --> 00:54:01,350
and it's communication heavy.

963
00:54:01,350 --> 00:54:04,570
It needs an all-to-all,
every MoE block.

964
00:54:04,570 --> 00:54:06,730
And since it has
different data, it

965
00:54:06,730 --> 00:54:08,570
needs to all reduce gradients.

966
00:54:08,570 --> 00:54:11,770
And there is a
CPU-GPU sync, which

967
00:54:11,770 --> 00:54:13,850
slows down your
training very much

968
00:54:13,850 --> 00:54:15,990
to launch this batch operation.

969
00:54:15,990 --> 00:54:22,730
So in summary, it is only to be
used for MoE trainings and/or--

970
00:54:22,730 --> 00:54:25,730
so the practical
solution that people

971
00:54:25,730 --> 00:54:28,210
do to train with
expert parallelism

972
00:54:28,210 --> 00:54:30,110
is to combine it with PPE.

973
00:54:30,110 --> 00:54:31,650
And how do they do that?

974
00:54:31,650 --> 00:54:35,170
So we said that the dispatch
and combine are slow.

975
00:54:35,170 --> 00:54:38,970
And if you remember, for our
one forward, one backward,

976
00:54:38,970 --> 00:54:42,150
we can overlap the
forward and the backward.

977
00:54:42,150 --> 00:54:45,250
So what we do here is-- so
we have two batches, which

978
00:54:45,250 --> 00:54:47,050
is the blue and the green here.

979
00:54:47,050 --> 00:54:50,570
So when we want to do
the dispatch, which

980
00:54:50,570 --> 00:54:55,660
is for the blue
batch, in parallel,

981
00:54:55,660 --> 00:54:58,520
we can do the backward
for the other batch.

982
00:54:58,520 --> 00:55:00,320
And this is nice because--

983
00:55:00,320 --> 00:55:03,540
so MLP here doesn't
need this batch.

984
00:55:03,540 --> 00:55:05,380
And we can do the dispatch here.

985
00:55:05,380 --> 00:55:08,720
And once this is done,
we can do the dispatch

986
00:55:08,720 --> 00:55:11,000
for the other batch, et cetera.

987
00:55:11,000 --> 00:55:13,140
And this is already
implemented in some libraries.

988
00:55:13,140 --> 00:55:17,420
For example in Megatron,
you can use these two flags.

989
00:55:17,420 --> 00:55:20,980
So in a nutshell, you can
combine all five parallelisms.

990
00:55:20,980 --> 00:55:23,680
And you're going to have
different communications

991
00:55:23,680 --> 00:55:28,060
in different steps
of your training.

992
00:55:28,060 --> 00:55:33,720
And we've seen that CP and
EP-- so EP only applied to MoEs

993
00:55:33,720 --> 00:55:36,240
and it only touches the MoE.

994
00:55:36,240 --> 00:55:41,560
And CP only touches
self-attention here.

995
00:55:41,560 --> 00:55:46,600
And yeah, all parallelism
are orthogonal.

996
00:55:46,600 --> 00:55:50,840
So you can combine
all five of them.

997
00:55:50,840 --> 00:55:55,640
And yeah, as a recap, we've
seen the five parallelisms.

998
00:55:55,640 --> 00:55:58,460
And you can find a
cheat sheet that we

999
00:55:58,460 --> 00:56:01,220
made in the Ultra-scale
Playbook on how

1000
00:56:01,220 --> 00:56:06,860
to make decisions following
the number of GPUs you have.

1001
00:56:06,860 --> 00:56:10,380
And finally, if you're more
interested in the infra

1002
00:56:10,380 --> 00:56:13,220
side of things, you can take
a look at the small training

1003
00:56:13,220 --> 00:56:15,400
playbook in the infra section.

1004
00:56:15,400 --> 00:56:19,020
We've made a lot of
benchmarks on how infra can

1005
00:56:19,020 --> 00:56:22,420
handle these types of workload.

1006
00:56:22,420 --> 00:56:26,340
And you can find more references
in the two books mentioned.

1007
00:56:26,340 --> 00:56:31,340
Also, the Jax Scaling Book
for TPU point of view.

1008
00:56:31,340 --> 00:56:35,660
And if you're interested
in these kind of things,

1009
00:56:35,660 --> 00:56:37,980
contributions are
very welcome, so

1010
00:56:37,980 --> 00:56:40,020
Nanotron, Torchtitan,
Megatron-LM,

1011
00:56:40,020 --> 00:56:42,900
and a lot of other libraries.

1012
00:56:42,900 --> 00:56:46,840
So feel free to reach out
if you want to contribute.

1013
00:56:46,840 --> 00:56:50,460
And one last thing is as we
scale to thousands of GPUs,

1014
00:56:50,460 --> 00:56:55,260
let's be mindful of their energy
impact and use them responsibly.

1015
00:56:55,260 --> 00:56:55,820
Thank you.

1016
00:56:55,820 --> 00:57:00,200

1017
00:57:00,200 --> 00:57:01,000
Thank you.

1018
00:57:01,000 --> 00:57:01,760
OK.

1019
00:57:01,760 --> 00:57:05,160
We'll take any questions
in person or over Zoom.

1020
00:57:05,160 --> 00:57:06,500
Any in the room?

1021
00:57:06,500 --> 00:57:15,640

1022
00:57:15,640 --> 00:57:17,720
Yeah, thanks for the talk.

1023
00:57:17,720 --> 00:57:20,220
It seems that for
expert parallelism,

1024
00:57:20,220 --> 00:57:25,940
you also have to have the tokens
routed evenly across ranks.

1025
00:57:25,940 --> 00:57:29,060
Because if all of your tokens
end up routed to one GPU,

1026
00:57:29,060 --> 00:57:31,560
then everything else is idle.

1027
00:57:31,560 --> 00:57:36,160
What are ways to fix
this in practice?

1028
00:57:36,160 --> 00:57:38,640
That's what the load
balancing tries to do.

1029
00:57:38,640 --> 00:57:43,080
So depending on which approach,
for example, Deepseek,

1030
00:57:43,080 --> 00:57:47,400
they initially introduced
the load balancing loss.

1031
00:57:47,400 --> 00:57:49,040
Sorry.

1032
00:57:49,040 --> 00:57:51,200
So the load balancing
acts basically

1033
00:57:51,200 --> 00:57:56,830
in the loss, a
factor that penalizes

1034
00:57:56,830 --> 00:58:00,072
uneven distribution of tokens.

1035
00:58:00,072 --> 00:58:01,530
And there's multiple
ways to do it.

1036
00:58:01,530 --> 00:58:03,670
There's the auxiliary
loss free where you just

1037
00:58:03,670 --> 00:58:06,330
add a bias term when
you compute the router,

1038
00:58:06,330 --> 00:58:11,070
and it would automatically
adjust the distribution

1039
00:58:11,070 --> 00:58:13,730
so that it's even across GPUs.

1040
00:58:13,730 --> 00:58:22,198

1041
00:58:22,198 --> 00:58:26,710
We also have some on Slido.

1042
00:58:26,710 --> 00:58:29,830
One is, do different parallelism
strategies meaningfully

1043
00:58:29,830 --> 00:58:33,070
affect scaling efficiency model
performance or convergence,

1044
00:58:33,070 --> 00:58:36,650
or do they just enable
reaching larger scales?

1045
00:58:36,650 --> 00:58:39,630
What's the first one,
scaling convergence?

1046
00:58:39,630 --> 00:58:41,830
Efficiency.

1047
00:58:41,830 --> 00:58:45,150
Convergence efficiency.

1048
00:58:45,150 --> 00:58:50,530
I think they would like scaling
laws of language models.

1049
00:58:50,530 --> 00:58:51,030
OK.

1050
00:58:51,030 --> 00:58:54,210
So actually, the
different policies

1051
00:58:54,210 --> 00:58:57,870
do not change the
forwards and backwards.

1052
00:58:57,870 --> 00:59:02,990
So ideally, if everything
is implemented correctly,

1053
00:59:02,990 --> 00:59:06,250
it should have the same
effect as if you had one GPU.

1054
00:59:06,250 --> 00:59:08,610
So like the same
scaling laws applies.

1055
00:59:08,610 --> 00:59:14,250
And you shouldn't find any
difference in using PP over EP

1056
00:59:14,250 --> 00:59:19,670
from computation point of view.

1057
00:59:19,670 --> 00:59:26,770

1058
00:59:26,770 --> 00:59:30,270
Another is, how can we reduce
GPU idle time during, say,

1059
00:59:30,270 --> 00:59:33,570
RL training when CPU side
checkpointing or environment

1060
00:59:33,570 --> 00:59:36,810
data processing becomes
sequential and a bottleneck?

1061
00:59:36,810 --> 00:59:37,890
Yeah.

1062
00:59:37,890 --> 00:59:42,450
So for that, at
least for text, you

1063
00:59:42,450 --> 00:59:46,630
can allocate a lot of
workers beforehand.

1064
00:59:46,630 --> 00:59:48,210
So the PyTorch
data loaders, they

1065
00:59:48,210 --> 00:59:51,290
allow you to specify the number
of workers that you can use,

1066
00:59:51,290 --> 00:59:53,910
and they can
pre-process the data

1067
00:59:53,910 --> 00:59:56,890
before the training workload.

1068
00:59:56,890 --> 01:00:00,870
So that should at least help
alleviate the pre-processing

1069
01:00:00,870 --> 01:00:03,590
so that it's done before you
even go through the training

1070
01:00:03,590 --> 01:00:04,450
iterations.

1071
01:00:04,450 --> 01:00:09,270

1072
01:00:09,270 --> 01:00:10,490
Any more in the room?

1073
01:00:10,490 --> 01:00:13,910

1074
01:00:13,910 --> 01:00:16,230
Also, someone asked, do
we have an automatic way

1075
01:00:16,230 --> 01:00:18,990
to decide the best parallelism?

1076
01:00:18,990 --> 01:00:21,130
Yeah, there have been
some papers for that.

1077
01:00:21,130 --> 01:00:24,170
I think if you take
a look at Jax book,

1078
01:00:24,170 --> 01:00:27,350
they've played with
that and dilated

1079
01:00:27,350 --> 01:00:31,770
the TPU decide on what's
the best parallelism.

1080
01:00:31,770 --> 01:00:35,370
But it really depends
on your specific.

1081
01:00:35,370 --> 01:00:37,810
So model size, how
many data you have,

1082
01:00:37,810 --> 01:00:41,670
the global batch size,
and the network you have.

1083
01:00:41,670 --> 01:00:46,430
So the biggest factor in what
we've said when you scale,

1084
01:00:46,430 --> 01:00:48,760
of course, is the
network bottleneck.

1085
01:00:48,760 --> 01:00:50,720
So if you have, for
example, let's say NVLink.

1086
01:00:50,720 --> 01:00:53,980
So in GPUs, usually
NVLink is either

1087
01:00:53,980 --> 01:00:58,820
four, eight, or for the most
recent one, 32 or even 72.

1088
01:00:58,820 --> 01:01:00,220
So it depends on that.

1089
01:01:00,220 --> 01:01:04,580
You can even use the more
communication heavy forms

1090
01:01:04,580 --> 01:01:08,360
of parallelism or the less
communication heavy ones.

1091
01:01:08,360 --> 01:01:11,660
And for example, for GPUs,
since they are in pods of 32,

1092
01:01:11,660 --> 01:01:13,220
usually people, they
allow themselves

1093
01:01:13,220 --> 01:01:17,940
to use tensor parallelism
over the 32 pods.

1094
01:01:17,940 --> 01:01:20,220
So yeah, it really
depends on the hardware

1095
01:01:20,220 --> 01:01:23,440
and the global batch size.

1096
01:01:23,440 --> 01:01:35,140

1097
01:01:35,140 --> 01:01:35,640
All right.

1098
01:01:35,640 --> 01:01:36,660
Thanks, guys.

1099
01:01:36,660 --> 01:01:40,240
Thanks so much Nouamane for
the amazing insightful talk.

1100
01:01:40,240 --> 01:01:43,570
So let's give it a hand
again for our speaker.

1101
